{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}Submit an extension - extdir{% endblock %}
{% block canonical %}<link rel="canonical" href="{{ url('submission_new') }}">{% endblock %}
{% block meta_description %}Point extdir at an open-source Shopware 6 extension the crawlers have not found.{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
<div class="wrap page page--narrow">
    <div class="page-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Missing something?</p>
        <h1>Submit an extension</h1>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
        {# The honest version of "why isn't my plugin here". Each channel has a blind
           spot, and a maintainer who hits one should be told which, not left guessing
           whether they were rejected. #}
        <p class="lead">
            Extensions are found on Packagist, through GitHub topics like
            <code>shopware6</code>, and by repository search. One that misses all three is
            invisible. If you know of one, this is the way in.
        </p>

        <form method="post" action="{{ path('submission_submit') }}" class="report-form">
            <input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token('submit') }}">

            <p>
                <label class="label" for="url">Repository address</label>
                <input class="input" id="url" name="url" required
                       inputmode="url" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false"
                       placeholder="https://github.com/vendor/plugin">
                <span class="small subtle">
                    GitHub only for now. Other forges are indexed but not yet submittable.
                </span>
            </p>

            <p>
                <button class="btn btn--primary" type="submit">Check and add</button>
            </p>
        </form>

        <h2>What happens next</h2>
        <p>
            The repository's <code>composer.json</code> is read straight away, and accepted if
            its type is <code>shopware-platform-plugin</code> and it has a tagged release. That is
            the same test every crawled extension passes. Nothing waits on a human.
        </p>
        <p>Compatibility, licence and maintenance signals fill in at the next crawl.</p>

        <h2>This is not a claim of ownership</h2>
        <p>
            Pointing at a public repository asserts nothing about who holds the rights. To
            correct or remove an extension that is <em>yours</em>, use
            <a href="{{ path('my_extensions') }}">ownership verification</a>, which needs a
            sign-in and proof.
        </p>
        <p>
            Nothing is copied. The code, releases and images stay on your forge. The
            <a href="{{ path('takedown') }}">takedown and correction policy</a> covers removal.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>
{% endblock %}
